<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayasimha Siddharaja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1092">1092</a>–1142</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayasimha Siddharaja was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilavada</a> (Patan) from 1092 to 1142. He defeated the Paramaras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and extended Chaulukya power to its height. A patron of learning, he supported the Jain scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a>, and built the Sahastralinga tank at Patan. He was succeeded by Kumarapala.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jayasimha was the son of the Chaulukya king Karna and his Kadamba queen Mayanalladevi. The 14th-century author Merutunga has him seated on the throne at the age of three, on 7 January 1094, which would place his birth in 1091, but the story is absent from earlier writers and does not seem accurate. His courtier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a> relates that Karna's nephew Devaprasada burned himself on Karna's funeral pyre. The historian A. K. Majumdar thinks this hides an unpleasant truth: that Devaprasada tried to usurp the throne from the young king and was killed by Mayanalladevi and her minister Santu, after which she ruled as regent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Literary sources and inscriptions agree that he defeated Khangara, also called Navaghana, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>; his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahod">Dahod</a> inscription boasts that he imprisoned that king. He appointed Sajjana governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girnar">Girnar</a>, which an inscription of 1120 corroborates, but he could not take all of Khangara's lands.</p><p>Inscriptions disagree on whether the Paramara king he defeated was Naravarman or his successor Yashovarman. Majumdar holds that the war began under the first and ended under the second, and dates the capture of the Paramara capital Dhara to 1135-1136; the title Avantinatha, Lord of Avanti, first appears in an inscription of 1137. The Shakambhari Chahamana king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnoraja">Arnoraja</a>, whom several sources say he subdued and to whom he gave his daughter Kanchana in marriage, helped him in this war, as did Asharaja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadol">Naddula</a>. Chaulukya chronicles claim that he then subdued the Chandela king Madanavarman, while a Chandela inscription claims the reverse, which suggests an inconclusive war.</p><p>He was a Shaivite who tolerated other faiths. Jains held important posts under him, and the Muslim historian Muhammad Aufi records that he inquired in person at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Khambhat</a> after a mosque was destroyed and eighty Muslims killed, and paid for its rebuilding. He renovated or rebuilt the Rudra Mahalaya Temple at Siddhapura, completed in 1142. The Sahastralinga lake was a renovation of one built by his ancestor Durlabha, ringed by 1008 small shrines each holding a linga. The grammar he commissioned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a>, the Siddha Hema Shabdanushasana, carries his name in its title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jayasimha had no son. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a> he learned by divination that his grand-nephew Kumarapala would succeed him; later Jain chronicles say that he hated Kumarapala and persecuted him. It is not known how long he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. Bohra and Satpanth traditions claim that he was converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, but there is no evidence that he ever gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jayasimha Siddharaja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jayasimha Siddharaja</b> and Kumarapala ruled at the height of Chaulukya power; the Jain scholar Hemachandra served their courts, and Kumarapala became a patron of Jainism.</blockquote><a href="/state/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Patan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.85,72.12&amp;year=1117" aria-label="Patan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6471 1285 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Jayasimha Siddharaja"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6921 1555h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6921 1555h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=23.85,72.12&amp;year=1117">What was here in 1117? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
